@author "Claude (Anthropic)"

; Fisher's waterbomb base on the top layer alone: "folding a 2:1 rectangle
; vertically in half then creating a waterbomb base on the top face only
; leaving the bottom face unfolded" [fisher1994, §5.3]. The rectangle of
; the standard library, corners .a to .d, folds
; in half along --spine, the right half over the left. The diagonals and the
; median parallel to the spine are marked on the top layer only, and the fan
; keeps the sector along the spine (ADR 0037): no hinge joins the moving
; pieces of the two layers, so the tip is the top layer, and the bottom layer
; stays one face. The top layer lies face down after the fold in half, so the
; letters of a waterbomb base seen from above read inverted on it: mountain
; diagonals and a valley median lay the flaps on top of the stack.

paper rectangle 2 1
fold (map .a onto .b) (moving .b) as --spine
.s0 = --spine * --ab
.s1 = free on --spine from .s0 at 1
mark (through .a .s1) (on #[.b]) as --d1
mark (through .s0 .d) (on #[.b]) as --d2
mark (map .a onto .s0) (on #[.b]) as --v
.m = --d1 * --d2
flatten (--d1 & .b mountain) (--d1 & .s1 mountain) (--d2 & .s0 mountain)
  (--d2 \ .s0 mountain) (--v & --ab valley) (--v \ --ab valley) (staying .s0 .s1)

; assert steps = 2
; assert faces = 7
; assert .a = (0, 0)
; assert .d = (0, 1)
; assert .b = (1, 0)
; assert .m = (1/2, 1/2)
